the US is removing China from a list of  countries it accuses of manipulating  their currencies it's happening just  days before they're expected to sign the  first stage of a trade deal  US President Donald Trump has long  accused China of keeping the one at low  levels to get an unfair trade advantage  Alan Fisher is in Washington and tells  us what this could mean for the trade  war go back four years Donald Trump when  he was campaigning to become president  said that in his first day in the White  House he would declare China to be a  currency manipulator well he eventually  got around to it but it was just five  months ago now it really was a symbolic  gesture it didn't really have much  impact it could have opened China up to  greater scrutiny from the International  Monetary Fund but it didn't really  happen this was at the height of Donald  Trump's trade war you'll remember he  imposed tariffs on billions of dollars  worth of Chinese goods he said that  China was pouring billions of dollars in  fees into the US Treasury in fact if you  look at a report that came out just last  week it said that 100% of the cost of  tariffs was being borne by American  families one survey said  American families were about eight  hundred dollars a year worse off so this  shows that things between China and the  United States are getting better and the  markets have responded well to that it's  up again in the United States at the  close of business on Monday so it seems  as if things are improving but there is  criticism of Donald Trump not  surprisingly it comes from the Democrats  Chuck Schumer is the leader of the  Democrats in the Senate he said look  China is still a currency manipulator  the United States shouldn't be taking  that tab away and it says we're giving  away a lot and not getting a lot in  return calling into question whether or  not Donald Trump really is the great  negotiator that he says he is Katrina Yu  is in Beijing and she has more on the  reaction there one way to describe it is  that China in the US had a rocky stormy  relationship and this  this currency manipulator announcement  this phase one trade deal is one bright  spot it's a break in the rain it's a  break in the lightning in the storm but  this is not the short answer is no this  is not the end to the trade one here the  state media and what we're reading  analysts here also believe that this  trade war is going to last for months if  not years because we've seen that you  know these trade talks have been going  on we've had more than a dozen talks  between Chinese and US negotiators and  they almost collapsed last year so we do  get the sense that phase one was really  the low-hanging fruit the things that  the US and China were able to agree on  and that China was able to happily agree  on and everything that they weren't so  sure they basically just push that to  phase two  but phase two they're going to have to  discuss the real things that are really  at the heart of this trade war that are  getting to the US the technology race  for example some structural changes that  China has pretty much we have said know  been very reluctant to change so there  is quite a lot of pessimism as to  whether we will even get to a Phase two  let alone a Phase three but for now I  think China at least the state media  here are focusing on the fact that look  this is not the end but we're still  working on it and that's a good thing so  so far that's that's what we've seen  from Beijing  [Music]
 

